    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.ai\r\n\r\n### Description\r\n\r\nAdd a function to easily register new textobjects like one can do in [vim-textobj-user](https://github.com/kana/vim-textobj-user).\r\n\r\nI'm writing a plugin that works on text objects for which I've defined a mini.ai compatible function. This makes it possible for users to use this textobject outside the confines of the plugin. The way I'm doing it right now is as follows:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n-- the config for mini.ai has already happened before\r\nlocal nn = require \"notebook-navigator\" -- <- my plugin\r\nnn.setup({})\r\n_G.MiniAi.config.custom_textobjects.h = nn.notebook_navigator_textobject\r\n```\r\n\r\nand it works just fine but I think it would be even nicer to be able to do something like:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal ai = require \"mini.ai\"\r\nlocal nn = require \"notebook-navigator\"\r\nai.register_textobject(\"h\", nn.notebook_navigator_textobject)\r\n```\r\n\r\nI think that this kind of functionality would also promote the development of a mini ecosystem of user defined textobjects [like you get with vim-textobj-user](https://github.com/kana/vim-textobj-user/wiki#general-purpose-text-objects).\r\n\r\nI wouldn't mind working on a PR if this feature is deemed appropriate for inclusion into mini.ai\r\n\r\n--------\r\n\r\nEdit:\r\nTL;DR The way of doing things described above by changing `_G.MiniAi` will cause you grief. Don't do it and instead follow  the advice from @echasnovski  below.\r\n\r\nModifying `_G.MiniAi.config` in your configs may uncover non deterministic behaviour from your package manager if you are not extra careful. For it to work you would have had to run the setup for \"mini.ai.setup\" or otherwise `_G.MiniAi` simply won't exist that much is obvious. If you use lazy.nvim it would seem like putting \"echasnovski/mini.ai\" on the dependencies would suffice but that's not quite true by itself. If for example your configuration for 'mini.ai' uses  'mini.nvim' (and then calls `require \"mini.ai\"`) as for example in:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n{\r\n  \"echasnovski/mini.nvim\",\r\n   config = function()\r\n     local ai = require \"mini.ai\"\r\n     ai.setup({})\r\n  end\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n but your dependencies on lazy, as stated above are for \"mini.ai\", as in:\r\n ```lua\r\n{\r\n  \"GCBallesteros/NotebookNavigator.nvim\",\r\n  dependencies = {\"echasnovski/mini.ai\"},  -- <--- DANGER!\r\n  config = function()\r\n    local nn = require \"notebook-navigator\"\r\n    nn.setup({})\r\n    _G.MiniAi.config.custom_textobjects.h = nn.notebook_navigator_textobject\r\n  end\r\n}\r\n```\r\nthen lazy won't respect the dependency (because it's not really there) and your config will fail on startup randomly.",
